A||||9||Wolfhound||Speed vs RPM||||5||
Z||000000||Wolfhound||12-05-1999||06:24 AM||||I wonder about the accuracy of my tach. For those of you with the 5 speed 2.8, does 2200 rpm at 60 mph sound correct for 5th gear? Also the engine missfires around 3500 rpm. any clues. Timings correct (set with emc disabled) and pickup coil, distributor cap and rotor, plugs and plug wires replaced. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||12-05-1999||03:33 PM||||2200 at 55-60mph is around noromal for many 4-5 speed trannys depending on final gearing. Engine size is only semi relivant for that issue.<P>As engine RPM increases Dwell time drops. Stock coils need a certain amount of dwell. As they age this requirement increases slightly. Next step is replacing the coil. Beyond that you may need to use something like one of MSD's aftermarket units. Get the MSD box, and it's optional wiring harness. The harness option allows the box to connect with no cutting of the OEM harness. But try just replacing the coil with a good after market one first. Accell and MSD both make coils for the Fiero V6.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||12-05-1999||04:49 PM||||Thank Ogre, I,ll give that a shot next. Runs fine to to 3500 then the bottom falls out so to speak.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||12-06-1999||06:54 AM||||Have you checked to see that your catalytic converter isn't partially blocked or that your fuel filter is plugging?  These will also cause high rpm problems.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||12-06-1999||11:50 AM||||Carroll, I Checked that from info on a search on this forum. Surprise! It,s been removed by previous owner. Could this have an effect?||||reg||5
Z||000005||Shaun41178||12-07-1999||04:22 PM||||Make sure you have no water in your gas tank.  for around a dollar you can get some drygas from your local autoparts store.  Put it in the gastank when you fill up.  <BR>If you have water in the tank then this can cause some problems that you mention.<P>i had this problem.  under normal driving ther was no difference but when I got on it it had a horrible misfire from 3700 rpm on up.<P>For a dollar it is worth it to try instead of a new coil that will cost around $35||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||12-07-1999||06:29 PM||||I don't believe the lack of a cat will have a negative effect on your performance.<P>When my wife and I returned from the Daytona show in 98 we caravaned with two 5 speed GTs and compared rpms with my v-6 4spd.  At 70 my 4spd Muncie turns 3000 rpm.  The 5spd Getrags were turning around 2200.<P>As far as the misfire at 3500 take a look at your plugs, wires, etc.  Also if you can see it, look at your timing indicator bracket on the front of the timing chain cover and see if it is loose or if it looks like it has moved.  My harmonic balancer was coming apart and the outer ring had moved back and was cutting the timing mark bracket in half.  If the harmonic balancer outer ring has moved you might have lost your accurate timing mark reference meaning that even though the timing is set according to the marks it could be way off.<P>Sorry for the rambling but it's been a long day and I'm ready to go to the hotel.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000007||Wolfhound||12-07-1999||06:59 PM||||I,ll try the water remover,It won't hurt. My ignition wires are new Accell 8.8 Distributor and rotor (new) (brass contacts)Pickup coil (new).<BR>  I,ll find TDC and check the timing mark. Thats a good shot. And a new coil is the next shot. Hope to take care of it this weekend.<BR>Thanks again for all the input everyone.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
Z||000008||RFMike||12-21-1999||09:28 PM||||Carroll,<BR>I've seen rpm/speed numbers before for the 4 speed Muncie.  I have an 86 GT with a 4 speed, and at 3k I'm doing 78 mph.  This seems different from others I have seen posted.  Did Pontiac make more than one rear end for the 86 GT?||||reg||1
